  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="ddd8d7a4-d23a-4d5a-b35d-49e504aa12a4"><problem><para>
      <para>Use the following scenario to answer questions 1 – 3.</para>
      <para>A water balloon is launched by a catapult into the air. The water balloon is launched from 4 feet above the ground, with an initial vertical velocity of 40 feet per second.</para>
      <para>The function \(h(t)=-16t^2 + 40t + 4\) models the height of the water balloon over the ground, in feet, \(t\) seconds after launch.</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="A DOWNWARD PARABOLA. X-AXIS LABEL IS TIME IN SECONDS AND Y-AXIS LABEL IS HEIGHT IN FEET. THE GRAPH PASSES THROUGH THE POINTS (0, 4), (0.5, 20), (1, 28), (1.5, 28), (2, 20), AND (2.5, 4)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/796834755c9625082b6b6d76fe2c1c69b585dd60"/></media></figure></para>
      <para>What is the height of the water balloon 1 second after launch?</para>
      </para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>18 feet</para><para>22 feet</para><para>28 feet</para><para>34 feet</para></problem><solution><para>28 feet</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is 28 feet. </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
